ICPs can be made either as highly crystalline structures, often referred to as metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), or amorphous structures. In addition, they can be made in macroscopic forms or as dispersible nano- and microparticles, which have properties distinct from their macroscopic counterparts. Our group and several others have focused on the development of synthetic methods for these ICP particles. Unlike their bulk counterparts, these structures can be dispersed in many solvents, have well-defined shapes and sizes, and exhibit properties such as rapid ion exchange kinetics, which are often a function of their nano- and microscale architectures. The goal of this review is to describe the synthetic approaches, characterization methods and scope of utility of these novel structures.
## Scope and vocabulary
Historically, crystalline MOFs were studied for their structural diversity and later their high porosities, which could impact on both catalytic and gas storage applications.12,13 Typically, in MOF synthesis, a combinatorial approach is used to identify the appropriate solvent and temperature conditions required to obtain a pure crystalline product.14 In 2005, we reported the development of conditions for arresting the polymerization process at early stages to generate ICP particles with micro- and even nanoscale dimensions.1 These methods, which typically involve solvent-induced precipitation, do not favor slow crystallization and therefore often result in an amorphous structure or a crystalline phase distinct from the thermodynamically-favored MOF. Consequently, these structures often have properties that are significantly different from their MOF counterparts.
The nomenclature for this class of materials is not straightforward. It is somewhat confusing and a misnomer to refer to these structures as MOFs, since many of them lack crystallinity and therefore a well-defined framework. Consequently, we refer to these structures as ICP particles. Therefore, ICP particles include a variety of crystalline, amorphous and semi-crystalline coordination polymer materials, including examples with a clearly identifiable morphology (e.g., spheres, rods or cubes).
## Structures, synthesis and mechanistic insights Structures
Our group, and another group led by Dong and Wang, independently developed the first methods for spherical ICP particles.1,2 As proof-of-concept, our group utilized bifunctional BMSB (bis-metallo-tridentate Schiff base) ligands 1 (Fig. 1) and metal acetate precursors \((\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II}),\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)}\) to initiate ICP formation.1,15 The reaction was controlled and stopped at the nano- and microparticle stages by the use of solvent-induced precipitation. The as-made structures were spheres with smooth surfaces (Fig. 2A) and exhibited an average diameter between \(100\;\mathrm{nm}\) to several microns, depending on the specific reaction conditions. Dong et al. utilized pyphenylenediamine (2) and a hexachloroplatinate salt to prepare related structures and similar morphologies.2 Since this initial work, we and others have significantly extended the library of structures realized through this approach, and have made inroads into developing particles that exhibit nonspherical morphologies and morphology-dependent physical and chemical properties. For example, Jung and Oh have made cube-shaped ICP particles 3a from salen-based precursor 3 and a \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\) salt (Fig. 2B),16 and we have recently shown that microscopic rods 4b (Fig. 2C) can be made from carborane ligands 4 and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) under the appropriate reaction conditions (Fig. 3).17 Indeed, with this carborane ligand, we have shown that subtle changes in reaction conditions can be used to prepare three distinct morphologies: bulk macroscopic 3D MOF 4a (Fig. 3A), 3D MOFs in the form of crystalline microrods 4b (Fig. 3B) and amorphous agglomerates 4c (Fig. 3C). Lu et al. have utilized \(\mathrm{{Pt}(I I)}\) pincer complexes 5
