## Synthesis
There are three different well-established strategies for synthesizing ICP particles: (i) formation and precipitation of particles triggered by the introduction of a solvent into a solution of ligands and metal cations (Fig. 4), which causes precipitation of the particles as they form, (ii) the use of microemulsion techniques and (iii) solvothermal synthesis. In addition to these strategies, ICPs have been observed to form and precipitate spontaneously under certain conditions upon mixing metal cations and ligands.
Thus far, strategy one has been the most commonly used method for the synthesis of coordination polymer nano- and microparticles. Typically, in this method, the organic ligand and metal cation are dissolved in a solvent, in which they and the resulting ICPs are highly soluble, and then a second solvent is introduced into the system that induces the precipitation of the ICPs. The particles are then collected by centrifugation. The process is relatively rapid, usually complete within hours and can be reversed by the addition of an excess of the original solvent, one that often coordinates to the metal nodes and competes with the ligands involved in the polymerization. This method has been utilized with a variety of different ligands, such as metallated BMSB ligand 1,1,15 bifunctional salen ligand \(\mathbf{6}^{19}\) and Tr¨oger's base ligands 7,20 and a number of metal cations, including \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\) , \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) , \(\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) . In addition to its generality, this method provides control over the size of the particles through
subtle changes in the reaction conditions. For example, the rate of addition of the solvent has been shown to be inversely related to the size of the particles produced; relatively fast addition of the precipitation solvent (sometimes referred to as a ''poor'' or ''weak'' solvent) results in particles 1a (made from BMSB 1 (metallated with \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) ) and a \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\) salt) as small as \(190\,\pm\,60\,\mathrm{\nm}\) , whereas slow addition results in significantly larger particles (e.g. \(1.60~\pm~0.47~\,\upmu\mathrm{m})\) . Additionally, the polarity of the precipitation solvent also appears to influence the size of the particles formed: in one example (1a) when pentanes were used as the precipitation solvent, the particles formed were significantly larger \((780~\pm~230~\mathrm{{nm})}\) than the particles formed using diethyl ether \((190\,\pm\,60\,\mathrm{\nm})\) . Finally, judicious choice of the precipitation solvent can affect the morphology and crystallinity of the resulting particles. In the synthesis of 6-based particles with \(\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)}\) cations, the use of diethyl ether as the precipitation solvent resulted in the formation of large micron-sized amorphous spherical particles 6a, whereas the use of methanol resulted in the formation of crystalline rod-like structures 6b (Fig. 5). Moreover, the addition of methanol to 6a resulted in its solid-state conversion to crystalline rod structures 6b, illustrating the dynamic nature of this methodology.19
Although fewer examples of the microemulsion or solvothermal strategies exist, each represents a useful alternative
method for the synthesis of ICP micro- and nanoparticles. For example, Lin et al. used the water-in-oil microemulsion strategy to synthesize Gd(III) 8a (Fig. 2E), Gd(III)/doped\(\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{III})\) 8b and Gd(III)/doped-Tb(III) 8c ICP particles, and then evaluated them as MRI contrast agents.21 An important advantage of this method is that the resulting structures are crystalline, allowing for a better understanding of the molecular connectivity of these particles. In addition, varying the water/surfactant ratio in the microemulsion also allowed for some control over the size and morphology of the resulting nanostructures (Fig. 6C and 6D). More recently, a solvothermal synthetic strategy was used to make salen 3-based coordination polymer nanocubes 3a (Fig. 2B).16 In this strategy, the ligand and metal were dissolved in a mixture of DMSO/DMF and then heated, resulting in the formation of the nanocubes. Here, the size of the particles were controlled by varying the temperature of the reaction, with higher temperatures favoring larger particles. Additionally, the ratio of DMSO/DMF affected the size of the particles formed, with a higher DMSO content resulting in progressively larger cubes.
## Formation mechanisms
Several mechanistic studies have provided insight into how ICP particles form, which is crucial for developing better ways of controlling their size and shape. A challenge in studying ICP particles rather than MOFs is that one is often working with amorphous rather than crystalline structures, and therefore X-ray diffraction typically cannot be used to study the transformations that ultimately lead to the desired particles. Nevertheless, our group has used scanning electron microscopy (SEM) to map the particle growth process in
two different but related examples (Fig. 7). The first involves ligand 1 and a \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\) salt, while the most recent examines in great detail the polymerization of Tr¨oger's base precursors 7a and 7b with the same metal salt.1,20 Time-resolved SEM shows that the particle growth processes are qualitatively similar (Fig. 7A–D). In a typical mechanistic study of the ligand 7-based system, aliquots of the reaction mixture were analyzed
over the course of the process. Globular seeds are observed at the initial stages of particle formation, which then begin to agglomerate into spherically-shaped particles with rough surface topologies. Under magnification, one can see that these particles are composed of aggregates of linked \(\sim\!27\ensuremath{\,\mathrm{~nm~}}\) diameter nanoparticles. Over time, the surface of the particles become smooth, ultimately yielding almost perfect spheres with little apparent surface roughness.
From this analysis, we proposed a working mechanism (Fig. 7E) for the particle formation process. It involves nucleation, followed by oligomerization, aggregation of the particle seeds, fusion, growth and finally annealing. At high concentrations of ligand and metal ion, the first three steps are relatively fast. It is possible that the surfaces of the seeds contain many unreacted sites for further polymerization, allowing rapid fusion and growth via coordination chemistry. However, since the small particles can reduce the surface tension through agglomeration in the presence of the precipitation solvent, this driving force cannot be completely discounted. Over time, as the concentration of ligand and metal ion precursors decrease, the nucleation of new particles is diminished, and the growth and annealing of existing nuclei become dominant, resulting in spherical ICP particles with smooth surfaces.
Jung and Oh observed a somewhat similar process and proposed a related growth mechanism.16 Interestingly, in their system, the initial seed particles were nanowires rather than cubes. The nanowires aggregated into rough cubic agglomerates, which annealed into nanocubes with smooth surfaces. The authors did not provide an explanation as to why cubes rather than larger nanowires formed in this aggregation and subsequent annealing process. This is, perhaps, one of the most important limitations of the field as it stands today. There are some gross generalities one can use to describe many particle growth processes, but there is very little information about how and why one particle morphology or shape forms as opposed to another.
## Shape regulation
Since ICP microparticles are typically formed by slow diffusion of an initiation or precipitation solvent into a solution containing the particle precursors, the formation rate of metal–ligand coordination polymers is often greater than the rate at which the molecules can organize into an ordered crystalline state. Crystalline MOFs in the majority of cases are not dynamic structures, and once made cannot be re-transformed into different macroscopic structures. This constitutes a major difference between MOFs and the vast majority of organic polymers, which easily reorganize upon changes to external conditions (solvent, pH, temperature, pressure, etc.). Therefore, in some respects, many ICP particles (especially amorphous ones) resemble classical polymers more than MOFs, but in contrast with organic polymers, one can use the coordination sites of ICP particles as a powerful way of controlling their 3D structures, and chemical and physical properties, even after particle formation.
For example, spherical ICP microparticles 6a have been prepared from a mixture of 6 and \(\mathrm{{Ni}(n)}\) acetate precursors via a solvent-induced precipitation method.19 Upon thermal evacuation, the 6a particles of lose pyridine but retain their spherical shape (Fig. 5). A spectroscopic investigation of this system showed that the polymer backbone stays intact under these conditions, but that pyridine is released from the nickel sites. When a new solvent (methanol) is added to the particles, a significant morphological change takes place, resulting in crystalline rods 6b. Characterization of these rods, including a single crystal X-ray diffraction study, shows that the polymer particles and rods have the same chemical connectivity in terms of the polymer backbone but different ancillary ligands on the \(\mathrm{{Ni}(n)}\) center. This system underscores how relatively
small changes in the coordination chemistry of the metal nodes can have significant effects on the morphology of the resulting materials. This process is pseudo-reversible, allowing one to chemically toggle between morphologies by using different coordinating ligands.
There are several examples where the introduction of a new ancillary ligand or counterion results in significant and reversible morphological changes. For example, Che et al. recently reported a counterion-induced process that results in the conversion of initially formed nanowires into wheel-like structures (Fig. 2D).18 The initial wire structures are made from a Pt pincer complex, 5, capable of forming \(\mathrm{Pt-Pt}\) bonds during the polymerization process. When either acetonitrile or \(\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{\mathrm{~-~}}\) is used to replace \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\)ligands on the platinum sites, the conversion is initiated and takes place over a few hours. The authors attribute the conversion from wires to wheels to a combination of changes in columbic repulsion, due to the formation of structures with neighboring cations, that result in increased curvature and eventually the wheel motif. The realization of such sophisticated structures emphasizes the design elements possible for ICPs. Indeed, the goal is not simply to generate aesthetically pleasing structures but rather provide ways of controlling morphology in a meaningful and predictable manner. Such capabilities will substantially increase the tool kit available to the nanoscientist and chemist for rationally making useful materials.
## Optical and magnetic properties
In addition to controlling the morphology of ICP particles, one can also control their optical and magnetic properties through the choice of starting material (metal and ligand
precursors), ancillary ligands and post-modification processes. For example, a recently reported valence tautomeric system demonstrates how the magnetic properties of certain ICPs may be modulated through low spin to high spin transformations. Vigorous stirring of a solution of 3,5-di-tert-butyl-1,2-catechol (9a), 1,4-bis(imidazol-1-ylmethyl)benzene (9b) and a \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) acetate salt at room temperature, followed by the addition of a precipitation solvent, results in the formation of particles 9c (Fig. 2F). The association of an electroactive building block with an adequate bridging ligand creates ICP particles that exhibit valence tautomeric properties. The magnetic properties of these nanoparticles were directly measured in an aqueous colloidal suspension, which shows reversible temperature-dependent interconversion from the low spin to the high spin isomer. Such transformations may be useful in the development of magnetic switches and relevant detection systems.
Another example of ICPs with tailorable properties involves particles made from BMSB ligand 1. We found that adding different solvents to a dispersion of ICP particles 1a enabled us to tune the optical properties of these particles. Different solvents induced ligand exchange at the metal sites, resulting in significant optical changes, which could be observed spectroscopically or with the naked eye (Fig. 8). For example, a red toluene suspension of the spherical particles turned yellow when methanol was added to the reaction vessel. This color change results from the replacement of the pyridine ligand on the \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) metal centers of 1a with methanol. If the solvent is removed from this complex and the particles are redispersed in toluene with DMSO, the yellow solution turns red due to the formation of \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) –DMSO adducts. It is well known that isoelectronic monomers of this class of compounds exhibit characteristic red-shifts in their solution state as a function of the increasing \(\sigma\) -donor properties of the ancillary ligands. As a consequence, the optical properties of the ICP microparticles are highly dependent upon the type and effects of different coordinating ligands, such as DMSO, pyridine, DMF, acetone and methanol (Fig. 8C, left to right). All reactions are completely reversible and can be effected within seconds of introduction to the appropriate small molecule. Since one can easily control the size of these particles and their corresponding optical properties, they may one day serve as a new class of diagnostic labels for various detection applications.
## Porosity
Partial evidence of the porosity of certain ICP structures has come from ion exchange with systems based on 1. For example, polymer particles 1a, consisting of 1 with \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\) metal incorporated into the ligand pockets, can be successfully exchanged with \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) by stirring a solution of dispersed particles with copper acetate at room temperature for \(30\,\mathrm{\min}\) , resulting in particles 1b. During the ion exchange process (Fig. 9), the particles retain their shape and size. However, their fluorescence is quenched due to the \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) ions, which replace the \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\) ions coordinated to the tridentate Schiff base ligands. Note that exchange does not occur at the carboxylate nodes. This type of exchange process works for other salts, including \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\) 1c, \(\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{II})\) 1d and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{II})\) 1e, although in some cases one needs to use an excess of metal ion to effect complete exchange (interior and exterior sites). Finally, the overall exchange rate is inversely dependent upon the size of the particle.
Since the ion exchange reactions suggest that structures based upon 1 are microporous, the gas sorption properties of similar structures based on 6 were studied. Interestingly, although the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) BET (Brunauer–Emmett–Teller) calculated surface area of the spherical ICPs based on 6 after evacuation at \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum suggests a relatively small surface area \((\sim\!20\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) compared to many MOF structures, their \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake properties are similar to zeolites (ZSM-5: \(0.71\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) ). The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake ability of these particles was also measured (areas \(\sim\!240\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ) and is significantly greater than \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) . This gas molecule selectivity may be due in part to the average pore size distribution of the ICP particles, which is too small to allow diffusion of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) into the micropores of the particles.
A related system composed of Tröger's base-derived ligand 7 (Fig. 3) shows even higher hydrogen uptake and selectivity, presumably due to its higher total pore volume and surface area (Fig. 10). When compared to 7a, ligand 7b leads to particles with a higher internal surface area due to its larger size and bent shape (which minimizes \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking). Both families of spherical structures exhibit isosteric heats of adsorption of approximately \(8\ \mathrm{kJ\mol}^{-1}\) , indicating strong physisorption of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to the internal pores of the particles.
A recent study addressing a comparison of bulk MOFs and ICP particles derived from \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) and carborane 4
precursors,17 showed how subtle changes in conditions can lead to materials with dramatically different morphologies and gas uptake properties. In this study, we discovered that slow
