Most recently, Oh et al. studied the gas sorption properties of crystalline micro rods based on 8 and an In(III) salt.26 Interestingly, the growth of these rods, 8d, can be controlled through a solvent facet blocking approach (using pyridine), providing materials with different shapes but nearly identical chemical connectivity. This was in part supported by the identical gas sorption properties \( (\mathrm{N}_{2} \) BET surface areas \( \sim\!1730\;\mathrm{~m}^{2}\;\mathrm{~g}^{-1} \) of the hexagonal rod and hexagonal disc analogs. In contrast to the example with carborane ICPs, the macroscopic morphology of 8d does not affect the molecular connectivity, and thus the porosity, of the resulting structures.
In addition to microporosity, ICP structures also can exhibit macroporous behavior. Indeed, Shen et al. recently employed amino acid aspargine (10) building blocks and lanthanide ions to create materials with macroscopic pores by a hydrothermal synthesis.27 These materials exhibited meso/macroporous structures, as observed by SEM and measured gas sorption (apparent surface area of \( \sim\!45\;\mathrm{~m}^{2}\;\mathrm{~g}^{-1} \)). SEM analysis of the products formed at different time points during this process showed the initial formation of elongated micrometer size spheres, which upon continued heating, fused together and generated larger structures with macroporous channels. In some respects, these materials can be viewed as "overcooked" versions of conventional ICP particles.
## Emerging applications
ICPs have several properties that conventional MOFs do not exhibit. The most obvious one is a clear shape and size identity that can be tailored from nano through to the microscopic length scales. Consequently, these materials can be easily dispersed in a wide variety of media and used for several applications where bulk MOFs are not suitable. For example, some ICPs are being explored as probes in imaging applications and as drug delivery materials. Lin et al. have reported a series of studies that focus on ICPs composed of lanthanides (termed NMOFs: nanoscale metal-organic frameworks) that exhibit promise for several biomedical applications.28 In particular, gadolinium-based ICPs 8a have been explored as magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) contrast agents (Fig. 6). The surfactant synthesis approach, starting from benzenedicarboxylate ligand 8, yielded nano-sized ICPs 8a, consisting of repeating \( (\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{BDC})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}) \) units. These nanorods with a \( 40\;\mathrm{nm} \) diameter and \( 100\;\mathrm{nm} \) length have a spin lattice relaxation parameter \( (R_{1}) \) 10 times that of conventional clinically used gadolinium liposomes.29 Interestingly, as the size of the particle decreases, the \( T_{1} \) increases, which was attributed to the
Gd(III) surface sites on the particles. Lin and co-workers have further expanded this approach via surfactant-mediated methods using 11 as a ligand, which significantly improved the size and morphology control, providing access to pyramid-and rectangular-shaped particles, as well as to rods of various dimensions.30 Finally, these studies were recently followed up by the same authors in an effort to improve on the problems that arise from gadolinium toxicity and the aqueous instability of the particles.31,32 A similar approach was implemented as a whole, but Gd(III) was substituted for the less toxic \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\), using 8 as a ligand. Additionally, the particles were coated with silica, which significantly slowed down the leaching of \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\), as a consequence of particle dissolution in the buffer medium. These advances make such systems more viable for in vivo studies.
Many ICP particles can be depolymerized rapidly by the addition of a solvent or ligand that competitively binds to the metal nodes. Crystalline MOFs tend to be substantially more stable and resistant to such processes. This characteristic of ICPs, coupled with size control, makes them attractive for drug delivery, as well as biological label applications. Lin et al. demonstrated that coating particles with silica prevents fast depolymerization in a buffer medium, which makes lanthanide systems based on 8 suitable for intracellular drug delivery by slowing down the rate of depolymerization.33 Most recently, Lin et al. demonstrated a platinum-based structure that can be utilized as an anti-cancer drug release system.34
Reacting a \(\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{III})\) salt with cis,cis,trans-(diamminedichlorodisuccinato)Pt(IV) ligand (12) leads to ICPs that can be isolated by solvent-induced precipitation. These particles were also encapsulated in amorphous silica to prevent their fast dissolution in the buffer medium. By varying the thickness of the silica coating of the particle, the depolymerization and release rates could be tailored. Functionalization of these particles with the cyclic peptide sequence C(RGDFK) lead to particles that are recognized by human colon (HT-29) cancer cells. Once inside the cells, the coordination particles depolymerize and release the \(\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})\) drug. Importantly, in principle, this approach can be generalized for other drugs, and used for studying and potentially treating a variety of different diseases.
Self-supported heterogeneous catalyst development is another example of an emerging research area where ICP particles may prove to be important.35 The internal structure of the ICPs, including pore dimensions, length and chirality, could provide advantages with respect to making targeted reactions more substrate-selective. Sweigart et al. have reported an example of a heterogeneous catalyst based on ICPs. These structures were prepared from a combination of \([\eta^{6}-1,4\)-hydroquinone/Rh(COD)] (13) (COD: cyclooctadiene) and \(\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{O}i\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Pr})_{3}\) in tetrahydrofuran (Fig. 11), and investigated for their catalytic activity with respect to the polymerization of phenylacetylene.36 When 13 is reacted with \(\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{O}i\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Pr})_{3}\), the deprotonation of its \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups occurs, during which time the \(\eta^{6}\)-hydroquinone moiety is converted into a \(\eta^{4}\)-quinone moiety. The authors found that the resulting heterogeneous ICP catalyst, 13a, produced a much longer poly(phenylacetylene) than 13 and exhibited high stereoselectivity. Under appropriate conditions, a \(48:2\) ratio of cis- to trans-poly(phenylacetylene) was formed with the ICP particles, while the
homogeneous catalyst exhibited very little selectivity under similar conditions. As the diameter of the heterogeneous catalyst was decreased, longer poly(phenylacetylenes) were obtained. As the solvent volume was increased, the poly(phenylacetylene) chain lengths decreased dramatically, consistent with the heterogeneous nature of the reaction. The stereoselective nature of this catalysis may result from the less flexible geometries of the catalytic sites compared to the analogous homogeneous system.
## Conclusions and outlook
Compared to the field of MOFs, which has been developed over the past two decades, the field of ICPs is at a nascent stage. That said, it is clear that ICPs represent significant opportunities as well as provide major challenges in many areas of research. Their tailorability in terms of composition, size and shape make them attractive for many applications where conventional MOFs are not suitable or ideal due to their static structures and bulk size. In this regard, a variety of biologically-relevant imaging, labelling, sensing and delivery systems have been envisioned. Significantly, ICPs are constructed from molecular, rather than atomic, building blocks, in contrast with their more conventional nanoparticle analogues (e.g., noble metal nanoparticles and semiconductor quantum dots).37,38 Therefore, one can begin to rationally design delivery and diagnostic probe systems where their functionality is derived from the materials contained within the particle, in addition to the ligands that comprise the surface. It is likely that the future will see many new types of diagnostic labels, amplification systems and therapeutics based upon ICPs, which will take advantage of their dynamic structures, morphologically-defined properties and compositional diversity.39
ICPs may also play a significant role in the development of new catalysts, and gas sorption and separation materials. Here, their dispersity, ease of synthesis, high surface areas in certain cases and the wide range of ligands one can employ to prepare them make ICPs extremely attractive for these applications.
To fully realize the technological potential of ICPs, significant fundamental advances are also required. Methods for controlling particle size are still fairly rudimentary, and shape control is predominately a product of serendipity rather than design. The issue of porosity control, which is central to the field of MOFs, can also be important in ICPs. Methods for controlling porosity in ICPs are even less developed than for MOFs. One of the reasons for this deficiency is the challenge of characterizing ICP structures. Their amorphous and dynamic structures, in many cases, preclude the use of X-ray diffraction methods to characterize them, therefore creating a constant challenge for the researcher to develop efficient methods for correlating their structure and function.
